# crontab — real-world examples

## Field format

```sh
# ┌ minute (0-59)
# │ ┌ hour (0-23)
# │ │ ┌ day of month (1-31)
# │ │ │ ┌ month (1-12)
# │ │ │ │ ┌ day of week (0-7, Sunday = 0 or 7)
# * * * * *  command_to_execute
```

## Example entries

```sh
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/bin:/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/sbin:/home/david/bin

# Every 15 minutes
*/15 * * * * /home/david/bin/command.sh

# Every hour on the hour
0 * * * * /home/david/bin/command.sh

# Every Saturday at 8:05 AM
5 8 * * 6 /home/david/bin/command.sh

# Shortcuts instead of the five fields
@reboot   /home/david/bin/on-boot.sh
@daily    /home/david/bin/nightly.sh
@monthly  /home/david/bin/report.sh
```

## Homelab: nightly rsync backup to the media server

```sh
# 2:30 AM every night, log output
30 2 * * * rsync -a /home/david/Public/ david@mediaserver:/srv/backups/public/ >> /home/david/.local/backup.log 2>&1
```

## Homelab: sync cheatsheets repo every morning

```sh
0 7 * * * cd /home/david/Public/ireaneus-cheatsheets && git pull --ff-only
```

## Read every user's crontab

```sh
for user in $(cut -f1 -d: /etc/passwd); do echo "$user"; crontab -u "$user" -l; done
```

## System cron with a user field (/etc/cron.d/*)

```sh
# /etc/cron.d/stat — sixth column is the user to run as
MAIL="david"
*/1 * * * * david sh /home/david/bin/stat.sh
```

## Mail control

```sh
MAILTO="david"   # send job output to david
MAILTO=""        # discard output mail
```

## Install a crontab from a file (replaces existing entries!)

```sh
crontab cron-file.txt
crontab -l          # verify what's installed
```

## Access control

If `cron.allow` exists, only listed users may use crontab. Otherwise,
anyone not in `cron.deny` may. If neither exists, only root can.

| Flag | What it does                    |
| ---- | ------------------------------- |
| -l   | list current crontab            |
| -e   | edit crontab in $EDITOR         |
| -r   | remove crontab (no confirm!)    |
| -u   | act on another user's crontab   |
